# OpenAgent — TODO

Check off only work covered by tests or a documented manual verification.

## v0.2.2 — Windows/browser follow-up
- [x] Prevent synthetic Windows discovery tests from reading real Registry installations.
- [x] Deduplicate a portable Chrome executable advertised as both Chrome and Chromium.
- [x] Remove blocked input thread from interactive login; clean up on Ctrl+C.
- [x] Add offline tests for keyboard cancellation and profile/browser cleanup.
- [x] Document Google security restrictions without attempting evasion.
- [ ] Verify `browser open` Enter and single Ctrl+C exits cleanly on Windows.
- [ ] Verify whether a selected site's login is permitted in the chosen browser.

## v0.2.1 — Browser discovery and selection
- [x] Offline discovery of standard Chrome, Edge, Firefox, and matching Playwright cache builds.
- [x] Allow portable browser folder or executable via `--browser-path`.
- [x] Persist selected backend without storing credentials.
- [x] Support Playwright Firefox as a separate browser engine and profile.
- [x] Provide explicit interactive `browser open` for one-time sign-in.
- [x] Differentiate native Firefox installation from compatible Playwright Firefox.
- [x] Test discovery and launch argument selection with offline fakes.
- [x] Verify manually downloaded Chromium on user's Windows machine (probe succeeded).
- [x] Verify Playwright Firefox installation and startup on Windows (probe succeeded).
- [ ] Verify Firefox profile persistence for a site that permits automated-browser login.

## v0.2.0 — Browser Manager
- [x] Separate a browser manager from service/model adapters.
- [x] Support dedicated persistent profiles and local CDP attachment.
- [x] Enforce tab ownership, page limits, and per-adapter navigation origins.
- [x] Represent startup, stopped, connected, failed, and disconnected states.
- [x] Handle navigation failure without leaving a managed tab open.
- [x] Require explicit transport restart after disconnection; do not replay requests.
- [x] Make Playwright optional and mock-test the browser lifecycle.
- [x] Use Windows-friendly default port 12321 and reserved-port fallback.
- [x] Document Windows setup and browser probes.
- [ ] Run `browser probe` in headed and CDP modes on user's Windows machine.
- [ ] Confirm Python 3.10 install and v0.2 full test suite on Windows.

